Firmware bug, patched for the host build only — fix it upstream and delete this file.

CanManager_new() sets every canMessageHistory[] slot to NULL and then, a
few lines later (and again in CanManager_send), writes through those
pointers. On the XC2000 that is a silent write into low memory; on the
host it is a segfault the moment the CAN manager is constructed, i.e. the
first cycle after the boot sequence. The loop also runs to 0x7FF
inclusive on an array of 0x7FF entries (one pointer past the struct).

Allocating a zeroed node per slot gives the code the storage it assumes.
The firstTimeMessage path in CanManager_send becomes unreachable, which
only means a first message is sent by the max-time rule instead.

--- a/canManager.c
+++ b/canManager.c
@@ -49,9 +49,9 @@
 {
     CanManager* me = (CanManager*)malloc(sizeof(struct _CanManager));
 
-    for (ubyte4 id = 0; id <= 0x7FF; id++)
+    for (ubyte4 id = 0; id < 0x7FF; id++)
     {
-        me->canMessageHistory[id] = 0;
+        me->canMessageHistory[id] = (AVLNode*)calloc(1, sizeof(AVLNode));
     }
 
     me->sendDelayus = defaultSendDelayus;
